This program presents an implementation of the Split Variational Autoencoder (SplitVAE) for disentangling structured latent representations from noisy(observed) and noiseless(model) trajectory data in the Minimum Threat Exposure Problem.
SplitVAE uses a dual-latent-space architecture with separate encodings for noisy (z₁) and noiseless (z₂) features. 
It is trained on paired data: 200 noisy and 200 noiseless trajectories labeled accordingly.
A custom loss function selectively applies reconstruction loss to noisy samples, enforces KL divergence on both latent spaces, and regularizes the z₁ encoding for noiseless data using a tunable penalty.
The model is trained over 1000 epochs, then used to generate 1000 synthetic samples from the learned latent space. 
This disentangled representation allows for more controllable and interpretable generation of navigation trajectories under varying environmental conditions.
